<section id="when-upgrade">
<h2>When to Upgrade and What to Expect</h2>

<p>You do not need to upgrade on day one. Most websites should spend the first 1–3 months on the free tier — learning what questions visitors actually ask, refining the training data, and measuring engagement. Upgrade when you hit one of these triggers:</p>

<h3>Trigger 1: Message Limit Hit Consistently</h3>
<p>If you exceed the free message limit three months in a row, the traffic volume justifies the upgrade. The cheapest paid plan (typically $19–$49/month) provides enough message capacity for sites with up to 50,000 monthly visitors.</p>

<h3>Trigger 2: You Want Lead Capture</h3>
<p>This is the most common upgrade trigger for affiliate marketers. Once your content is generating consistent traffic, capturing email addresses from chatbot conversations directly feeds your email list. The incremental revenue from a single email subscriber typically exceeds the monthly chatbot cost within 3–6 months.</p>

<h3>Trigger 3: Multiple Sites or Chatbots</h3>
<p>If you run multiple affiliate sites or want separate chatbots for different product categories, the free tier (limited to one chatbot) forces the upgrade.</p>

<h3>Trigger 4: User Expectations Outgrow the Bot</h3>
<p>This is a quality trigger, not a volume trigger. When visitors start asking questions the bot cannot answer — and the answer exists in content you have not yet trained it on — the limitation is training sources, not messages. Upgrading lets you train on your full site, PDFs, and help docs, improving answer accuracy.</p>

<p><strong>What to expect after upgrading:</strong> The chatbot becomes a real tool, not a novelty. It captures leads. It answers edge-case questions. It integrates with your email platform. The monthly cost — $19–$99 for most tools' starter paid tiers — is offset by the leads captured, support time saved, and improved on-site engagement metrics.</p>
</section>

